Ghostfolio, patched so a per-file compiler can run it and ngp's type-check accepts it.

1. `export type` on one re-export statement, libs/common/src/lib/dtos/index.ts, written by
   TypeScript's own quick fix for its isolatedModules error (TS1205), via
   bench/real/isolated-modules-fix.mjs. A per-file compiler, which Vite is, can't tell that
   `export {SomeInterface} from './x'` names a type, compiles it to a runtime re-export of
   nothing, and Rolldown stops the build with "missing export". `nx build` emits with the whole
   program's types and never hits this.

2. `$any()` on two expressions in libs/ui/src/lib/page-tabs/page-tabs.component.html. ngp's
   type-check blocks report TS2774 ("This condition will always return true since this function
   is always defined") for `@if (tab.onClick)`, where `tab` is a union of a tab with `onClick`
   and a tab with `routerLink`. ngc's type-check blocks for the same template don't, and
   `nx build` passes. The Angular team has said ngp's output can surface a few type errors ngc
   misses; this is one, and whether it's right is between the two compilers. `$any()` is
   Angular's own way to take an expression out of type-checking, and changes nothing at runtime.

Both toolchains compile the patched files; `nx build` and `nx serve` measure the same sources.

